# Copy to .env and edit. Never commit .env — it is gitignored.
#
# Quote any value containing a space. setup.sh and provision.sh `source` this
# file, so an unquoted space is parsed as a command and the script dies.
#
# Values below are the Debian production defaults. To run on the macOS dev box,
# uncomment the MAC DEV block at the bottom; later assignments win, so it
# overrides everything above it. Porting to Debian = comment that block out again.

# ─── Identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Must match the `media` user created by setup.sh, which owns the media tree.
PUID=1000
PGID=1000
TZ=Africa/Johannesburg

# ─── Paths (host side only) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DATA_ROOT is the host path backing /data. The container side is ALWAYS /data
# in every service — see spec §4.1. Never parameterise the container side.
DATA_ROOT=/data
CONFIG_ROOT=/opt/mediaserver

# ─── Hardware transcoding ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# On the target:  getent group render
# Required — the stack refuses to start without it, so a missing value fails
# loudly rather than silently dropping Jellyfin to software transcoding.
RENDER_GID=

# ─── Network ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Address the service ports publish on. 0.0.0.0 is intended: spec §5.1 wants
# direct LAN access. The security boundary is no router port-forwarding plus
# UFW, not the bind address — see docs/decisions.md D1.
BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0

# Home LAN subnet, e.g. 192.168.1.0/24 — consumed by setup.sh's UFW rules.
# Spec §5.3 says deny inbound except SSH and Tailscale, but taken literally
# that also blocks §5.1's direct LAN access, including Infuse on the Apple TV
# reaching Jellyfin. Setting this allows the home network through while the
# internet stays blocked. Leave it blank for a strictly tailnet-only box.
LAN_SUBNET=

# Caddy is the remote-access path and is the ONE service restricted to the
# tailnet interface. On the target:  tailscale ip -4
CADDY_BIND_ADDR=
# The <host> part of <host>.ts.net, fully qualified.
CADDY_DOMAIN=CHANGEME.ts.net

# ─── Host ports ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Container-side ports are fixed; only these host-side ones move.
JELLYFIN_PORT=8096
PROWLARR_PORT=9696
SONARR_PORT=8989
RADARR_PORT=7878
BAZARR_PORT=6767
QBIT_PORT=8080
QBIT_TORRENT_PORT=6881
SAB_PORT=8085
CADDY_HTTP_PORT=80
CADDY_HTTPS_PORT=443

# ─── API keys ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pinned rather than read out of each app's UI. The *arrs accept their key as a
# runtime environment variable, so setting it here means the key is known before
# the container has ever started — which is what lets scripts/provision.sh wire
# the stack together, and what Recyclarr authenticates with.
#
# Generate three distinct 32-char hex values:
#   openssl rand -hex 16
#
# Or let the provisioner do it:  ./scripts/provision.sh --init-keys
#
# Treat these as passwords. A valid *arr API key is full control of that app.
SONARR_API_KEY=
RADARR_API_KEY=
PROWLARR_API_KEY=

# qBittorrent Web UI credentials. qBittorrent generates a random temporary
# password on every start until a permanent one is set; provision.sh sets this
# one, after which the temporary password stops being issued.
QBIT_USER=admin
QBIT_PASS=

# SABnzbd and Bazarr ship with NO web UI login at all. Both are worth closing:
# SABnzbd runs post-processing scripts (arbitrary execution, same class of risk
# as qBittorrent's external-program setting), and Bazarr has full write access
# to the library. provision.sh sets these; leaving them blank leaves the UIs
# open to anyone who can reach the port.
#
# Setting these does not affect Sonarr/Radarr, which authenticate to SABnzbd
# with its API key rather than these credentials.
SAB_USER=admin
SAB_PASS=
BAZARR_USER=admin
BAZARR_PASS=

# ─── Usenet provider (news server) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# The provider stores the actual articles; the indexers below only say where
# they are. Both are required — indexers alone download nothing.
#
# Defaults are Eweka's published settings. provision.sh warns loudly rather
# than failing silently if USENET_USER is blank.
USENET_HOST=news.eweka.nl
USENET_PORT=563
USENET_SSL=true
USENET_CONNECTIONS=20
USENET_USER=
USENET_PASS=

# ─── Usenet indexers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# API keys from each site's profile page. Blank keys are skipped, so you can
# add one now and the other later. Both are Newznab; Prowlarr has built-in
# definitions, so only the key is needed.
NZBGEEK_API_KEY=
NZBPLANET_API_KEY=

# ─── Torrent indexers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public, no account required, so provision.sh enables these by default.
# They carry the anime side of the library — Usenet covers anime poorly, so
# without them Sonarr will find almost nothing for an anime series.
#
# Comma-separated Prowlarr definition names. Note "AnimeTosho" and not
# "Anime Tosho": Prowlarr ships both, and despite its private label the former
# needs no credentials and works, while the semiprivate one fails to connect.
#
# Add private trackers through the Prowlarr UI — they need per-site credentials
# and are not worth templating here.
# Quote it — the value contains a space, and setup.sh/provision.sh source this
# file, where an unquoted space would be parsed as a command.
TORRENT_INDEXERS="Nyaa.si,SubsPlease,AnimeTosho,Tokyo Toshokan"

# ─── smartd alerting (consumed by setup.sh, not by compose) ──────────────────
# msmtp relays SMART alerts through this SMTP server. Without a working relay
# smartd alerts fail silently, which defeats the point of monitoring a
# single-drive array — see docs/decisions.md Q3.
ALERT_EMAIL=
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_FROM=


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# MAC DEV BLOCK — uncomment everything below to run on the dev workstation.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Layers the dev override on automatically, so `docker compose up -d` needs no
# flags on either machine.
#COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.dev.yml
#
# Unused in dev — the override strips Jellyfin's group_add entirely. It still
# needs a value because Compose interpolates before it merges override files.
#RENDER_GID=0
#
# DATA_ROOT/CONFIG_ROOT are ignored in dev: the override swaps every bind mount
# for a named Docker volume, so /data lands on real ext4 inside Docker's Linux
# VM. That is what makes the hardlink test meaningful here — macOS VirtioFS
# does not reproduce inode or permission behaviour faithfully.
#
# Keep everything on loopback; nothing dev-related should reach the LAN.
#BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
#CADDY_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
#CADDY_DOMAIN=localhost
#
# 8080 is commonly taken (patchwork, among others).
#QBIT_PORT=8081
#
# 80/443 need root on macOS.
#CADDY_HTTP_PORT=8880
#CADDY_HTTPS_PORT=8443
